## Wide and nested code fences (v0.4.0, #149)

A fence opens on a line of three-or-more backticks (or tildes) and closes only
on a line with a matching run of *at least as many* of the same character. A
wider fence can therefore wrap a narrower one verbatim — exactly what you need
to document fenced code that itself contains fences. No interpolation or
directives are parsed anywhere inside.

### A four-backtick fence wrapping a three-backtick block

````markdown
```python
print("{lang} is NOT interpolated in here")
```
````

The inner three-backtick line above does not close the four-backtick fence;
only a run of four-or-more backticks does. Back in the body, {{lang}} interpolates.

### Mismatched markers never cross-close

A tilde run never closes a backtick fence, so the tildes below stay literal:

```
~~~
{lang} stays literal — the tildes did not close the backtick fence.
~~~
```

After the backtick fence: {{lang}}.
